{"bendscript":"0.1","blocks":[{"id":"blk-98970","kind":"heading","level":1,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163359","text":"Skill 07 — Cognitive Science Foundations"}]},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98972","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163360","text":"Research grounding for each of the ten protocols: the theories, the papers, the design constraints, and the open questions. Eight cognitive primitives grounded in cognitive science / neuroscience, plus two cross-cutting protocols (OS-009 PRISM, OS-010 PULSE) grounded in psychometrics and closed-loop control theory."}]}],"id":"blk-98971","kind":"quote"},{"id":"blk-98973","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163361","text":"Why This Matters"}]},{"id":"blk-98974","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163362","text":"OpenSentience protocols are not arbitrary architecture decisions. Each one maps to a specific cognitive science finding about how intelligent systems manage knowledge, attention, and self-regulation. Understanding the research helps you understand "},{"id":"spn-163363","marks":["italic"],"text":"why"},{"id":"spn-163364","text":" the protocols work the way they do and where the boundaries of the analogy lie."}]},{"id":"blk-98975","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163365","text":"OS-001: Hippocampal Consolidation"}]},{"id":"blk-98976","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163366","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163367","text":" McClelland, McNaughton & O'Reilly (1995). \"Why there are complementary learning systems in the hippocampus and neocortex.\""}]},{"id":"blk-98977","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163368","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163369","text":" The brain uses two complementary systems for memory. The hippocampus rapidly encodes new experiences (fast write). The neocortex slowly integrates this knowledge into long-term structure (consolidation). Replaying memories during sleep transfers hippocampal traces into neocortical representations without catastrophic forgetting."}]},{"id":"blk-98978","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163370","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-001:"}]},{"id":"blk-98979","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98981","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163371","text":"New knowledge must be stored immediately without disrupting existing memory"}]}],"id":"blk-98980","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98983","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163372","text":"Background consolidation must merge, decay, and prune to maintain quality"}]}],"id":"blk-98982","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98985","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163373","text":"Episodic memories (events) are stored differently from semantic memories (facts)"}]}],"id":"blk-98984","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98987","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163374","text":"Confidence decays over time unless reinforced by retrieval or outcomes"}]}],"id":"blk-98986","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-98988","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163375","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-98989","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98991","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163376","text":"Immediate "},{"id":"spn-163377","marks":["code"],"text":"store_node"},{"id":"spn-163378","text":" writes (hippocampal fast-write analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-98990","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98993","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163379","marks":["code"],"text":"run_consolidation"},{"id":"spn-163380","text":" background cycle (neocortical integration analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-98992","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-98995","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163381","text":"Three node types mapping to memory systems: episodic, semantic, procedural"}]}],"id":"blk-98994","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-98996","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163382","text":"OS-002: Working Memory Gating"}]},{"id":"blk-98997","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163383","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163384","text":" O'Reilly & Frank (2006). \"Making working memory work: A computational model of learning in the prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia.\""}]},{"id":"blk-98998","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163385","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163386","text":" The prefrontal cortex maintains active representations in working memory, but not everything gets in. The basal ganglia acts as a gate — deciding what information is relevant enough to enter working memory based on learned relevance signals and conflict detection."}]},{"id":"blk-98999","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163387","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-002:"}]},{"id":"blk-99000","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99002","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163388","text":"Not all retrieved knowledge should be acted upon equally"}]}],"id":"blk-99001","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99004","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163389","text":"Cyclic knowledge (contradictions, self-reinforcing claims) needs detection"}]}],"id":"blk-99003","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99006","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163390","text":"The kappa parameter serves as the conflict signal: kappa = 0 means no"}]}],"id":"blk-99005","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99007","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163391","text":"conflict (open the gate), kappa > 0 means conflict (engage deliberation)"}]},{"id":"blk-99008","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99010","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163392","text":"Routing must be fast for the common case (acyclic) and thorough for the"}]}],"id":"blk-99009","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99011","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163393","text":"rare case (cyclic)"}]},{"id":"blk-99012","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163394","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-99013","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99015","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163395","text":"SCC detection on retrieved neighborhoods (basal ganglia conflict signal)"}]}],"id":"blk-99014","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99017","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163396","text":"Kappa computation (gating threshold)"}]}],"id":"blk-99016","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99019","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163397","text":"Fast vs deliberate routing based on kappa value"}]}],"id":"blk-99018","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99020","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163398","text":"OS-003: Dual-Process Theory"}]},{"id":"blk-99021","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163399","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163400","text":" Kahneman (2011). \"Thinking, Fast and Slow.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99022","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163401","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163402","text":" Human cognition operates in two modes. System 1 is fast, automatic, and heuristic-based — it handles routine decisions effortlessly. System 2 is slow, effortful, and analytical — it engages when System 1 encounters novelty, conflict, or high stakes."}]},{"id":"blk-99023","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163403","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-003:"}]},{"id":"blk-99024","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99026","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163404","text":"Most agent decisions should be fast (System 1 analog)"}]}],"id":"blk-99025","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99028","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163405","text":"Deliberation should engage only when triggered by conflict signals (kappa > 0)"}]}],"id":"blk-99027","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99030","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163406","text":"The deliberation protocol must structure argumentation (bid, debate, vote,"}]}],"id":"blk-99029","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99031","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163407","text":"commit) to resolve conflict rather than amplify it"}]},{"id":"blk-99032","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99034","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163408","text":"Timeout mechanisms prevent deliberation from stalling indefinitely"}]}],"id":"blk-99033","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99035","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163409","marks":["bold"],"text":"What AgenTroMatic implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-99036","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99038","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163410","text":"Four-phase deliberation pipeline (bid/debate/vote/commit)"}]}],"id":"blk-99037","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99040","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163411","text":"Argumentation framework with claims, warrants, and rebuttals"}]}],"id":"blk-99039","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99042","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163412","text":"Consensus and escalation mechanisms"}]}],"id":"blk-99041","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99043","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163413","text":"OS-004: Endogenous Attention"}]},{"id":"blk-99044","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163414","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163415","text":" Desimone & Duncan (1995). \"Neural mechanisms of selective visual attention.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99045","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163416","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163417","text":" Attention is not just stimulus-driven (exogenous — a loud noise grabs your attention). It is also goal-directed (endogenous — you look for your car keys because you intend to drive). Endogenous attention biases perception toward goal-relevant information, filtering out distractions."}]},{"id":"blk-99046","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163418","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-004:"}]},{"id":"blk-99047","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99049","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163419","text":"The attention engine must prioritize based on active goals, not just recency"}]}],"id":"blk-99048","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99050","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163420","text":"or novelty"}]},{"id":"blk-99051","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99053","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163421","text":"Salience scoring combines urgency, recency, goal relevance, and confidence"}]}],"id":"blk-99052","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99055","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163422","text":"The survey/triage/dispatch cycle mirrors the scan/filter/act structure of"}]}],"id":"blk-99054","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99056","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163423","text":"biological attention"}]},{"id":"blk-99057","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99059","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163424","text":"Dispatch modes (act, learn, escalate, idle) prevent attention from always"}]}],"id":"blk-99058","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99060","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163425","text":"demanding action"}]},{"id":"blk-99061","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163426","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-99062","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99064","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163427","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_survey"},{"id":"spn-163428","text":" (endogenous scan of goal-relevant state)"}]}],"id":"blk-99063","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99066","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163429","marks":["code"],"text":"attention_run_cycle"},{"id":"spn-163430","text":" (full survey/triage/dispatch loop)"}]}],"id":"blk-99065","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99068","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163431","text":"Goal bias in salience scoring"}]}],"id":"blk-99067","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99069","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163432","text":"OS-005: Resource Rationality"}]},{"id":"blk-99070","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163433","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163434","text":" Lieder & Griffiths (2020). \"Resource-rational analysis: Understanding human cognition as the optimal use of limited computational resources.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99071","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163435","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163436","text":" Optimal cognition is not about always computing the best answer. It is about allocating computational effort proportional to the value of the decision. Simple decisions warrant fast, cheap heuristics. High-stakes decisions justify expensive, thorough analysis."}]},{"id":"blk-99072","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163437","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-005:"}]},{"id":"blk-99073","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99075","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163438","text":"Three model tiers map to three levels of computational investment"}]}],"id":"blk-99074","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99077","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163439","text":"The same tool surface and topology are available at every tier"}]}],"id":"blk-99076","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99079","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163440","text":"Escalation rules define when to upgrade from cheap to expensive processing"}]}],"id":"blk-99078","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99081","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163441","text":"Budget constraints (tokens, latency, cost) prevent unbounded computation"}]}],"id":"blk-99080","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99082","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163442","marks":["bold"],"text":"What Graphonomous and Agentelic implement:"}]},{"id":"blk-99083","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99085","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163443","text":"local_small / local_large / cloud_frontier tier definitions"}]}],"id":"blk-99084","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99087","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163444","text":"Escalation thresholds based on confidence and decision stakes"}]}],"id":"blk-99086","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99089","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163445","text":"Token and latency budgets per tier"}]}],"id":"blk-99088","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99090","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163446","text":"OS-006: Executive Function"}]},{"id":"blk-99091","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163447","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163448","text":" Miyake, Friedman et al. (2000). \"The unity and diversity of executive functions and their contributions to complex frontal lobe tasks.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99092","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163449","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163450","text":" Executive function is an umbrella term for the cognitive processes that regulate, control, and manage other cognitive processes. Miyake et al. identified three core components: inhibitory control (suppressing inappropriate responses), task switching (flexibly shifting between tasks), and working memory updating (monitoring and revising held information)."}]},{"id":"blk-99093","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163451","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-006:"}]},{"id":"blk-99094","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99096","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163452","text":"Inhibitory control maps to the permission system (blocking disallowed actions)"}]}],"id":"blk-99095","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99098","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163453","text":"Task switching maps to the lifecycle state machine (managing agent state)"}]}],"id":"blk-99097","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99100","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163454","text":"Working memory updating maps to graduated autonomy (adapting the level of"}]}],"id":"blk-99099","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99101","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163455","text":"agent independence based on accumulated trust)"}]},{"id":"blk-99102","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99104","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163456","text":"The audit trail provides metacognitive monitoring — awareness of what the"}]}],"id":"blk-99103","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99105","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163457","text":"system has done"}]},{"id":"blk-99106","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163458","marks":["bold"],"text":"What `open_sentience` implements:"}]},{"id":"blk-99107","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99109","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163459","text":"PermissionEngine (inhibitory control)"}]}],"id":"blk-99108","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99111","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163460","text":"AgentLifecycle GenStateMachine (task switching)"}]}],"id":"blk-99110","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99113","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163461","text":"AutonomyController (working memory updating / cognitive flexibility)"}]}],"id":"blk-99112","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99115","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163462","text":"AuditWriter (metacognitive monitoring)"}]}],"id":"blk-99114","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99116","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163463","text":"OS-007: Adversarial Robustness — Self/Non-Self Discrimination"}]},{"id":"blk-99117","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163464","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theoretical grounding:"},{"id":"spn-163465","text":" Adaptive immunity theory (Burnet 1959; Janeway 1989)."}]},{"id":"blk-99118","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163466","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163467","text":" Biological immune systems distinguish self from non-self through a combination of innate pattern recognition and adaptive memory. The same architecture maps cleanly to agent threat detection: known-good behavior is \"self,\" novel attack patterns are \"non-self,\" and the system must learn to recognize new threats without misclassifying legitimate variation."}]},{"id":"blk-99119","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163468","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-007:"}]},{"id":"blk-99120","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99122","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163469","text":"Five threat categories with explicit detection rules"}]}],"id":"blk-99121","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99124","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163470","text":"Defense protocols must be reversible (quarantine before destroy)"}]}],"id":"blk-99123","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99126","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163471","text":"Integration with OS-006 for permission revocation and OS-008 for circuit"}]}],"id":"blk-99125","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99127","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163472","text":"breaking — the immune response is enacted by the governance and harness layers"}]},{"id":"blk-99128","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163473","text":"OS-008: Supervisory Attentional System"}]},{"id":"blk-99129","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163474","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key paper:"},{"id":"spn-163475","text":" Norman & Shallice (1986). \"Attention to action: Willed and automatic control of behavior.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99130","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163476","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163477","text":" Routine behavior runs automatically through contention scheduling between learned action schemas. A "},{"id":"spn-163478","marks":["italic"],"text":"supervisory attentional system"},{"id":"spn-163479","text":" intervenes when novel, dangerous, or constraint-violating situations arise. The supervisory system does not execute behavior directly — it modulates which schemas are allowed to run."}]},{"id":"blk-99131","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163480","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-008:"}]},{"id":"blk-99132","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99134","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163481","text":"The harness is the runtime that calls the agent, not a tool the agent calls"}]}],"id":"blk-99133","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99136","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163482","text":"It enforces pipeline ordering, quality gates, sprint contracts, and context"}]}],"id":"blk-99135","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99137","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163483","text":"management"}]},{"id":"blk-99138","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99140","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163484","text":"It intervenes when prerequisites are not met or when quality thresholds are"}]}],"id":"blk-99139","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99141","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163485","text":"not crossed — but it does not generate the agent's outputs"}]},{"id":"blk-99142","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163486","text":"OS-009: PRISM — Meta-Cognition + Psychometrics"}]},{"id":"blk-99143","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163487","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-99144","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99146","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163488","text":"Rasch, G. (1960). \"Probabilistic models for some intelligence and attainment"}]}],"id":"blk-99145","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99147","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163489","text":"tests.\" (Item Response Theory foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-99148","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99150","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163490","text":"Lord, F. M. (1980). \"Applications of item response theory to practical testing"}]}],"id":"blk-99149","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99151","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163491","text":"problems.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99152","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99154","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163492","text":"Green, D. M. & Swets, J. A. (1966). \"Signal detection theory and"}]}],"id":"blk-99153","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99155","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163493","text":"psychophysics.\""}]},{"id":"blk-99156","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99158","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163494","text":"Flavell, J. H. (1979). \"Metacognition and cognitive monitoring.\" (Meta-cognition)"}]}],"id":"blk-99157","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99159","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163495","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163496","text":" Measuring whether a system "},{"id":"spn-163497","marks":["italic"],"text":"learns"},{"id":"spn-163498","text":" (rather than merely "},{"id":"spn-163499","marks":["italic"],"text":"answers"},{"id":"spn-163500","text":") requires the same toolkit psychometricians built for measuring human learning: calibrated item difficulty, separation of item-quality from learner-quality parameters, and detection of response bias. PRISM applies IRT to scenario calibration and signal detection theory to dimension scoring, then closes the meta-cognitive loop by reflecting on its own scenarios and evolving them."}]},{"id":"blk-99160","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163501","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-009:"}]},{"id":"blk-99161","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99163","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163502","text":"9 continual-learning dimensions (retention, plasticity, transfer,"}]}],"id":"blk-99162","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99164","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163503","text":"contradiction handling, etc.) — each with calibrated scoring rubrics"}]},{"id":"blk-99165","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99167","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163504","text":"4-phase evaluation loop: compose → interact → observe → reflect → diagnose"}]}],"id":"blk-99166","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99168","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163505","text":"(the diagnostic must itself be a closed loop)"}]},{"id":"blk-99169","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99171","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163506","text":"BYOR ingestion — point PRISM at any repo and it generates scenarios"}]}],"id":"blk-99170","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99173","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163507","text":"IRT calibration of scenario difficulty across cycles"}]}],"id":"blk-99172","kind":"list-item"},{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99175","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163508","marks":["bold"],"text":"PULSE-aware:"},{"id":"spn-163509","text":" PRISM's "},{"id":"spn-163510","marks":["code"],"text":"interact"},{"id":"spn-163511","text":" phase reads any system's PULSE manifest"}]}],"id":"blk-99174","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99176","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163512","text":"at runtime and drives the inner loop through its declared phases"}]},{"id":"blk-99177","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163513","text":"OS-010: PULSE — Closed-Loop Control Theory + Temporal Cognition"}]},{"id":"blk-99178","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163514","marks":["bold"],"text":"Key references:"}]},{"id":"blk-99179","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99181","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163515","text":"Wiener, N. (1948). \"Cybernetics: or Control and Communication in the Animal"}]}],"id":"blk-99180","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99182","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163516","text":"and the Machine.\" (Closed-loop control foundation)"}]},{"id":"blk-99183","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99185","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163517","text":"Allen, J. F. (1983). \"Maintaining knowledge about temporal intervals.\""}]}],"id":"blk-99184","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99186","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163518","text":"(Interval algebra)"}]},{"id":"blk-99187","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99189","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163519","text":"CloudEvents v1 specification (CNCF, 2019). (Event envelope standard)"}]}],"id":"blk-99188","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99190","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163520","marks":["bold"],"text":"Theory:"},{"id":"spn-163521","text":" Wiener's cybernetics established that intelligent behavior — in animals or machines — requires closed feedback loops with explicit phases: sense, decide, act, observe, adjust. Allen's interval algebra formalized the 13 possible relationships between temporal intervals, providing a vocabulary for describing how loops can nest, overlap, and signal one another. PULSE combines these into a manifest standard: every loop in the [&] portfolio declares its phases in the same vocabulary, and cross-loop signals use CloudEvents v1 envelopes so that loops can compose without bespoke adapters."}]},{"id":"blk-99191","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163522","marks":["bold"],"text":"Design constraints for OS-010:"}]},{"id":"blk-99192","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99194","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163523","text":"5 canonical phase kinds ("},{"id":"spn-163524","marks":["code"],"text":"retrieve"},{"id":"spn-163525","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163526","marks":["code"],"text":"route"},{"id":"spn-163527","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163528","marks":["code"],"text":"act"},{"id":"spn-163529","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163530","marks":["code"],"text":"learn"},{"id":"spn-163531","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163532","marks":["code"],"text":"consolidate"},{"id":"spn-163533","text":")"}]}],"id":"blk-99193","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99195","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163534","text":"cover the closed-loop control archetype; custom phases extend it without breaking the schema"}]},{"id":"blk-99196","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99198","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163535","text":"5 canonical cross-loop tokens ("},{"id":"spn-163536","marks":["code"],"text":"TopologyContext"},{"id":"spn-163537","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163538","marks":["code"],"text":"DeliberationResult"},{"id":"spn-163539","text":","}]}],"id":"blk-99197","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99199","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163540","marks":["code"],"text":"OutcomeSignal"},{"id":"spn-163541","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163542","marks":["code"],"text":"ReputationUpdate"},{"id":"spn-163543","text":", "},{"id":"spn-163544","marks":["code"],"text":"ConsolidationEvent"},{"id":"spn-163545","text":") cover the observed inter-system signaling needs"}]},{"id":"blk-99200","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99202","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163546","text":"7 invariants (phase atomicity, feedback immutability, append-only audit,"}]}],"id":"blk-99201","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99203","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163547","text":"kappa routing, quorum before commit, outcome grounding, trace ID propagation) encode the structural correctness conditions"}]},{"id":"blk-99204","items":[{"blocks":[{"id":"blk-99206","kind":"paragraph","spans":[{"id":"spn-163548","text":"A 12-test conformance suite makes \"PULSE-conforming\" objectively verifiable"}]}],"id":"blk-99205","kind":"list-item"}],"kind":"list","ordered":false},{"id":"blk-99207","kind":"heading","level":2,"spans":[{"id":"spn-163549","text":"Open Research Questions"}]}